    { marks: 3, q: 'Explain the three-tier quasi-judicial machinery set up under COPRA, with its limits.',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'District level — up to ₹20 lakh (NCERT)', kw: ['district', '20 lakh'] },
        { text: 'State level — ₹20 lakh to ₹1 crore', kw: ['state', '1 crore'] },
        { text: 'National level — above ₹1 crore; 2019 Act raised limits', kw: ['national', '2019'] }
      ],
      model: '1. District level court: deals with cases where the claim is up to ₹20 lakh.\n2. State level court: deals with claims between ₹20 lakh and ₹1 crore, and hears appeals from District courts.\n3. National level court: deals with claims above ₹1 crore and hears appeals from State courts. (These are the NCERT figures. The Consumer Protection Act, 2019 raised the limits: District up to ₹1 crore, State ₹1 crore to ₹10 crore, National above ₹10 crore.)' },
    { marks: 4, q: 'CASE STUDY: Read the source and answer.\n"Reji Mathew, a healthy boy studying in Class XI, was suffering from tonsillitis. He was operated on in a private hospital under general anaesthesia. Due to improper anaesthesia he showed brain abnormalities and was crippled for life. His father filed a complaint of medical negligence. The State Commission dismissed it for insufficient evidence; on appeal, the National Commission held the hospital responsible and directed it to pay compensation."\n(a) Which kind of consumer exploitation is shown here? [1]\n(b) Which consumer right did Reji\'s father use? [1]\n(c) What does this case show about consumer courts? Give two points. [2]',
      keypoints: [
        { text: '(a) Medical negligence / deficiency in service', kw: ['negligence'] },
        { text: '(b) Right to seek redressal', kw: ['redress'] },
        { text: '(c) Services are covered; appeal possible; evidence needed, time-consuming', kw: ['appeal', 'evidence|time'] }
      ],
      model: '(a) Medical negligence — a deficiency in a service (hospital) that harmed the consumer.\n(b) The right to seek redressal (compensation for damage).\n(c) Consumer courts cover services like hospitals, and a consumer can appeal from the State to the National Commission if not satisfied. But the process needs strong evidence and takes a long time.' },
    { marks: 4, q: 'CASE STUDY: Read the source and answer.\n"When we buy a commodity, we find certain details on the packing: ingredients, price, batch number, date of manufacture, expiry date and the address of the manufacturer. Some products also carry logos like ISI, Agmark or Hallmark."\n(a) Which consumer right is reflected in the printing of these details? [1]\n(b) Which mark would you look for on a bottle of honey? [1]\n(c) Why are standardisation marks useful to consumers? Give two reasons. [2]',
      keypoints: [
        { text: '(a) Right to be informed', kw: ['inform'] },
        { text: '(b) Agmark', kw: ['agmark'] },
        { text: '(c) Assure quality and safety; help choose; compulsory for health-related goods', kw: ['quality', 'safe|health'] }
      ],
      model: '(a) The right to be informed (right to information).\n(b) Agmark, since honey is an agricultural product.\n(c) Standard marks assure consumers of the quality of goods, as producers can use them only if they meet set standards. They also protect health and safety — certification is compulsory for items like LPG cylinders, food colours and cement.' },
    { marks: 5, q: 'Mention some of the rights of consumers and write a few sentences on each.',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Right to safety — e.g. pressure cooker safety valve', kw: ['safety', 'cooker|lpg|electric'] },
        { text: 'Right to be informed — MRP, expiry, ingredients', kw: ['inform', 'mrp|expiry'] },
        { text: 'Right to choose — gas stove with connection', kw: ['choose|choice', 'gas|toothbrush'] },
        { text: 'Right to seek redressal — compensation', kw: ['redress', 'compensation'] },
        { text: 'Right to represent in consumer courts', kw: ['represent'] }
      ],
      model: '1. Right to safety: consumers must be protected from goods and services hazardous to life and property — e.g. a pressure cooker must have a working safety valve; LPG cylinders and electrical appliances must be safe.\n2. Right to be informed: consumers must get details of ingredients, MRP, batch number, manufacture and expiry dates and the manufacturer\'s address; they can protest if charged above MRP.\n3. Right to choose: no one can be forced to buy an extra product, e.g. a gas dealer insisting on selling a stove with a new connection, or toothpaste sold only with a toothbrush.\n4. Right to seek redressal: consumers can seek redressal against unfair trade practices and get compensation depending on the degree of damage.\n5. Right to represent: consumers can be represented in consumer courts, often through consumer forums.' },
    { marks: 5, q: 'Critically examine the progress of the consumer movement in India.',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Began in the 1960s; COPRA 1986 a major step', kw: ['1960', '1986|copra'] },
        { text: 'Three-tier courts, RTI, standard marks; 700+ groups, only 20–25 well organised', kw: ['700', '20'] },
        { text: 'Process cumbersome, expensive, time-consuming; lawyers needed', kw: ['expensive|costly', 'time'] },
        { text: 'Cash memos not issued, evidence hard; weak enforcement', kw: ['cash memo|bill', 'enforce'] },
        { text: 'Awareness spreading slowly; consumers must be active', kw: ['aware', 'active|participat'] }
      ],
      model: '1. The consumer movement began in the 1960s as a social force and its pressure led to the Consumer Protection Act (COPRA), 1986 — a major step.\n2. Progress: a three-tier system of consumer courts, the RTI Act 2005, standard marks and the 2019 Act. India has more than 700 consumer groups, but only about 20–25 are well organised and recognised.\n3. However, the redressal process is cumbersome, expensive and time-consuming, and consumers often need lawyers.\n4. In most purchases cash memos or bills are not issued, so evidence is hard to gather; laws, including those protecting unorganised workers, are weakly enforced and market rules are often not followed.\n5. Awareness is spreading slowly. The movement can move forward only with the active participation of consumers.' },
    { marks: 5, q: 'What legal measures have been taken by the government to empower consumers in India?',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'COPRA 1986 (replaced by Consumer Protection Act 2019)', kw: ['1986', '2019'] },
        { text: 'Three-tier consumer courts — District, State, National', kw: ['district', 'national'] },
        { text: 'Consumer rights: safety, information, choice, redressal, representation', kw: ['safety', 'choose|choice'] },
        { text: 'Right to Information Act 2005', kw: ['2005'] },
        { text: 'Compulsory standardisation — ISI, Agmark, Hallmark; MRP on packets', kw: ['isi|agmark|hallmark', 'mrp'] }
      ],
      model: '1. The Consumer Protection Act, 1986 (COPRA) was enacted, and it was replaced by the stronger Consumer Protection Act, 2019, which also set up a Central Consumer Protection Authority.\n2. A three-tier quasi-judicial machinery of District, State and National consumer courts gives simple and inexpensive justice.\n3. Consumers were given rights: right to safety, right to be informed, right to choose, right to seek redressal and right to represent in consumer courts.\n4. The Right to Information Act, 2005 lets citizens get information about the functioning of government departments.\n5. Standardisation and certification — ISI, Agmark, Hallmark — are compulsory for many goods, and packets must show MRP, ingredients and expiry date. 24 December is observed as National Consumers\' Day to spread awareness.' }
